window.BAHR_DATA=window.BAHR_DATA||{};window.BAHR_DATA["passages-106"]=[{"id":"K106.01","q":"106:1–4","s":106,"surah":"Quraysh","surah_ar":"قريش","title":"Quraysh · Heading, verses, and the governing construction","cairo":"K IV:800–802 · T XVI:585–590","source_page_first":800,"source_page_last":802,"chunk":"passages-106","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Heading, verses, and the governing construction [IV:800–801]","html":"<p>Meccan; four verses. [The DKA heading additionally places its revelation after al-Tīn.]\nIn the name of God, the All-Merciful, the Merciful.</p><p>For the accustomed security of Quraysh—(1) their accustomed winter and summer journey—(2) let them worship the Lord of this House, (3) who has fed them against hunger and secured them against fear. (4)</p><p>“For the accustomed security of Quraysh” (li-īlāfi (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">لإيلاف</bdi>) Quraysh) depends grammatically on “let them worship”: He commands them to worship Him because He has accustomed them to the two journeys. If you ask why fāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ف</bdi>) then enters, I reply: because the statement carries a conditional meaning. Its sense is: if for nothing else, let them worship Him for their accustomed security. God’s blessings upon them are innumerable; if they do not worship Him for His other blessings, let them worship Him for this one conspicuous blessing.</p><p>It is also said to mean: “Marvel at Quraysh’s accustomed security.” Another interpretation connects it to what precedes: “He made them like eaten stalks for the accustomed security of Quraysh.” This resembles enjambment (taḍmīn) in poetry: the meaning of one verse depends upon the preceding verse in such a way that it cannot stand correctly without it. In Ubayy’s codex the two are one sūrah, with no division. It is related of ʿUmar that he recited them both in the second unit of the sunset prayer, having recited “By the fig” in the first.</p><p>The meaning is that He destroyed the Abyssinians who intended to attack them, so that people would hear of this, stand in greater awe of them, and accord them increased respect. Thus security would be established for their two journeys and no one would dare attack them.</p>","author":"kashshaf","source_id":"K106.01"},{"type":"exposition","title":"The fāʾ and the implied condition [XVI:585–586]","html":"<p>Meccan; four verses. In the name of God, the All-Merciful, the Merciful.</p><p>On “why, then, does fāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ف</bdi>) enter?”: the fāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ف</bdi>) [in the question fa-lima] indicates objection. That is: if li-īlāf (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">لإيلاف</bdi>) depends on fa-l-yaʿbudū (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فليعبدوا</bdi>), why does the fāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ف</bdi>) of succession stand between the governing expression and what it governs? He answers that fāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ف</bdi>) introduces the consequence of an omitted condition. This must be supplied, because if one reconstructs simply “let them worship Him for the accustomed security of Quraysh,” the fāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ف</bdi>) remains without anything to which it relates.</p><p>It may also be understood as reinforcement, with fāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ف</bdi>) marking succession: “For Quraysh’s accustomed security, let them worship Him—so let them worship.” Likewise His saying “so let them rejoice” [10:58]. Its permissibility has already been transmitted from al-Zubayr, from al-Zajjāj. His saying “and your Lord—magnify Him” [74:3] follows this pattern. He says: “Fāʾ enters because of the conditional meaning, as though it said, ‘Whatever happens, do not cease to magnify Him.’”</p><p>On “the meaning is: immā (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">إمّا</bdi>) lā (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">لا</bdi>), let them worship Him”: the author is reported to have said, “The Arabs say ‘Do this, immā (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">إمّا</bdi>) lā (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">لا</bdi>),’ meaning ‘If you will not do something else, do this.’ Mā is additional, compensating for the omitted kāna. They pronounce lā (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">لا</bdi>) with imāla (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">إمالة</bdi>) because it stands in place of the verb, like balā, both taking the verb’s place. One also says, ‘Give me this, immā (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">إمّا</bdi>) lā (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">لا</bdi>).’”</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T106.01"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Linking the two sūrahs [XVI:586]","html":"<p>On “He made them like eaten stalks for the accustomed security of Quraysh,” al-Zajjāj says: “The meaning is: God destroyed the People of the Elephant so that Quraysh and their accustomed winter and summer journeys might remain.”</p><p>On “in the second [unit] of the sunset prayer”: that is, in the second rakʿa; in the first rakʿa he recited Sūrat “By the fig.” This apparently indicates that they are one sūrah. [This is Ṭībī’s inference, retained as his statement.]</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T106.02"}],"types":["exposition","language","theology","reports"],"ar":"مكية، وآياتها 4 «نزلت بعد التين» بسم الله الرحمن الرحيم\n\nلإيلاف قريش متعلق بقوله ليعبدوا أمرهم أن يعبدوه لأجل إيلافهم الرحلتين فإن قلت: فلم دخلت الفاء؟ قلت: لما في الكلام من معنى الشرط لأن المعنى: إما لا فليعبدوه لإيلافهم، على معنى: أن نعم الله عليهم لا تحصى، فإن لم يعبدوه لسائر نعمه، فليعبدوه لهذه الواحدة التي هي نعمة ظاهرة. وقيل المعنى: عجبوا لإيلاف قريش. وقيل: هو متعلق بما قبله، أى: فجعلهم كعصف مأكول لإيلاف قريش، وهذا بمنزلة التضمين في الشعر: وهو أن يتعلق معنى البيت بالذي قبله تعلقا لا يصح إلا به، وهما في مصحف أبى سورة واحدة بلا فصل. وعن عمر: أنه قراهما في الثانية من صلاة المغرب، وقرأ في الأولى: والتين . والمعنى أنه أهلك الحبشة الذين قصدوهم ليتسامع الناس بذلك، فيتهيبوهم زيادة تهيب، ويحترموهم فضل احترام، حتى ينتظم لهم الأمن في رحلتيهم، فلا يجترئ أحد عليهم.\n\nأريع آيات، مكية\n\nبسم الله الرحمن الرحيم\n\nقوله: (فلم دخلت الفاء)، الفاء دلت على الإنكار، أي: إذا كان \"لإيلاف\" متعلقاً بقوله \"فليعبدوا\"، فلِمَ دخلت فاء التعقيب بين العامل ومعموله؟ وأجاب أن الفاء جزاء شرط محذوف ولا بد من هذا التقدير؛ لأنه إذا كان التقدير: فليعبدوه لإيلاف قريش، تبقى الفاء\n\nولا متعلق لها. ويجوز أن يُحمل على التوكيد والفاء للتعقيب، كما يقال: لِئِلاف قريش ليعبدوه، فليعبدوا، وكذا قوله تعالى: ﴿فَلْيَفْرَحُوا﴾، وقد مر عن الزبير عن الزجاج جوازه، وعليه قوله تعالى: ﴿وَرَبَّكَ فَكَبِّرْ﴾ [المدثر: 3]، قال: \"دخلت الفاء لمعنى الشرط، كأنه قيل: وما كان فلا تدع تكبيره\".\n\nقوله: (لأن المعنى: إما لا فليعبدوه)، روي عن المصنف أنه قال: تقول العرب: افعل هذا إما لا، أي: إن كنت لا تفعل غيره فافعل هذا، و\"ما\" مزيدة، عوض من \"كان\" المحذوفة، وقد أمالوا \"لا\" لأنه ساد مسد الفعل كبلى، ولقيامهما مقام الفعل، ويقال: أعطني هذا إما لا.\n\nقوله: (فجعلهم كعصف مأكول لإيلاف فريش)، قال الزجاج: \"المعنى: أهلك الله أصحاب الفيل، لتبقى قريش وما قد ألفوا من رحلة الشتاء والصيف\".\n\nقوله: (في الثانية من صلاة المغرب)، أي: في الركعة الثانية، وفي الركعة الأولى سورة والتين، هذا ظاهر بأنهما سورة واحدة.","layers":[{"id":"K106.01","heading":"K106.01 — Heading, verses, and the governing construction [IV:800–801]","text":"Meccan; four verses. [The DKA heading additionally places its revelation after al-Tīn.]\nIn the name of God, the All-Merciful, the Merciful.\n\nFor the accustomed security of Quraysh—(1) their accustomed winter and summer journey—(2) let them worship the Lord of this House, (3) who has fed them against hunger and secured them against fear. (4)\n\n“For the accustomed security of Quraysh” (li-īlāfi (لإيلاف) Quraysh) depends grammatically on “let them worship”: He commands them to worship Him because He has accustomed them to the two journeys. If you ask why fāʾ (ف) then enters, I reply: because the statement carries a conditional meaning. Its sense is: if for nothing else, let them worship Him for their accustomed security. God’s blessings upon them are innumerable; if they do not worship Him for His other blessings, let them worship Him for this one conspicuous blessing.\n\nIt is also said to mean: “Marvel at Quraysh’s accustomed security.” Another interpretation connects it to what precedes: “He made them like eaten stalks for the accustomed security of Quraysh.” This resembles enjambment (taḍmīn) in poetry: the meaning of one verse depends upon the preceding verse in such a way that it cannot stand correctly without it. In Ubayy’s codex the two are one sūrah, with no division. It is related of ʿUmar that he recited them both in the second unit of the sunset prayer, having recited “By the fig” in the first.\n\nThe meaning is that He destroyed the Abyssinians who intended to attack them, so that people would hear of this, stand in greater awe of them, and accord them increased respect. Thus security would be established for their two journeys and no one would dare attack them.","title":"Heading, verses, and the governing construction [IV:800–801]","author":"kashshaf","surah":106,"ar":"مكية، وآياتها 4 «نزلت بعد التين» بسم الله الرحمن الرحيم\n\nلإيلاف قريش متعلق بقوله ليعبدوا أمرهم أن يعبدوه لأجل إيلافهم الرحلتين فإن قلت: فلم دخلت الفاء؟ قلت: لما في الكلام من معنى الشرط لأن المعنى: إما لا فليعبدوه لإيلافهم، على معنى: أن نعم الله عليهم لا تحصى، فإن لم يعبدوه لسائر نعمه، فليعبدوه لهذه الواحدة التي هي نعمة ظاهرة. وقيل المعنى: عجبوا لإيلاف قريش. وقيل: هو متعلق بما قبله، أى: فجعلهم كعصف مأكول لإيلاف قريش، وهذا بمنزلة التضمين في الشعر: وهو أن يتعلق معنى البيت بالذي قبله تعلقا لا يصح إلا به، وهما في مصحف أبى سورة واحدة بلا فصل. وعن عمر: أنه قراهما في الثانية من صلاة المغرب، وقرأ في الأولى: والتين . والمعنى أنه أهلك الحبشة الذين قصدوهم ليتسامع الناس بذلك، فيتهيبوهم زيادة تهيب، ويحترموهم فضل احترام، حتى ينتظم لهم الأمن في رحلتيهم، فلا يجترئ أحد عليهم.","html":"<p>Meccan; four verses. [The DKA heading additionally places its revelation after al-Tīn.]\nIn the name of God, the All-Merciful, the Merciful.</p><p>For the accustomed security of Quraysh—(1) their accustomed winter and summer journey—(2) let them worship the Lord of this House, (3) who has fed them against hunger and secured them against fear. (4)</p><p>“For the accustomed security of Quraysh” (li-īlāfi (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">لإيلاف</bdi>) Quraysh) depends grammatically on “let them worship”: He commands them to worship Him because He has accustomed them to the two journeys. If you ask why fāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ف</bdi>) then enters, I reply: because the statement carries a conditional meaning. Its sense is: if for nothing else, let them worship Him for their accustomed security. God’s blessings upon them are innumerable; if they do not worship Him for His other blessings, let them worship Him for this one conspicuous blessing.</p><p>It is also said to mean: “Marvel at Quraysh’s accustomed security.” Another interpretation connects it to what precedes: “He made them like eaten stalks for the accustomed security of Quraysh.” This resembles enjambment (taḍmīn) in poetry: the meaning of one verse depends upon the preceding verse in such a way that it cannot stand correctly without it. In Ubayy’s codex the two are one sūrah, with no division. It is related of ʿUmar that he recited them both in the second unit of the sunset prayer, having recited “By the fig” in the first.</p><p>The meaning is that He destroyed the Abyssinians who intended to attack them, so that people would hear of this, stand in greater awe of them, and accord them increased respect. Thus security would be established for their two journeys and no one would dare attack them.</p>","lemma":"","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/middle-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}},{"id":"T106.01","heading":"T106.01 — The fāʾ and the implied condition [XVI:585–586]","text":"Meccan; four verses. In the name of God, the All-Merciful, the Merciful.\n\nOn “why, then, does fāʾ (ف) enter?”: the fāʾ (ف) [in the question fa-lima] indicates objection. That is: if li-īlāf (لإيلاف) depends on fa-l-yaʿbudū (فليعبدوا), why does the fāʾ (ف) of succession stand between the governing expression and what it governs? He answers that fāʾ (ف) introduces the consequence of an omitted condition. This must be supplied, because if one reconstructs simply “let them worship Him for the accustomed security of Quraysh,” the fāʾ (ف) remains without anything to which it relates.\n\nIt may also be understood as reinforcement, with fāʾ (ف) marking succession: “For Quraysh’s accustomed security, let them worship Him—so let them worship.” Likewise His saying “so let them rejoice” [10:58]. Its permissibility has already been transmitted from al-Zubayr, from al-Zajjāj. His saying “and your Lord—magnify Him” [74:3] follows this pattern. He says: “Fāʾ enters because of the conditional meaning, as though it said, ‘Whatever happens, do not cease to magnify Him.’”\n\nOn “the meaning is: immā (إمّا) lā (لا), let them worship Him”: the author is reported to have said, “The Arabs say ‘Do this, immā (إمّا) lā (لا),’ meaning ‘If you will not do something else, do this.’ Mā is additional, compensating for the omitted kāna. They pronounce lā (لا) with imāla (إمالة) because it stands in place of the verb, like balā, both taking the verb’s place. One also says, ‘Give me this, immā (إمّا) lā (لا).’”","title":"The fāʾ and the implied condition [XVI:585–586]","author":"tibi","on":["K106.01"],"anchor":"K106.01","surah":106,"ar":"أريع آيات، مكية\n\nبسم الله الرحمن الرحيم\n\nقوله: (فلم دخلت الفاء)، الفاء دلت على الإنكار، أي: إذا كان \"لإيلاف\" متعلقاً بقوله \"فليعبدوا\"، فلِمَ دخلت فاء التعقيب بين العامل ومعموله؟ وأجاب أن الفاء جزاء شرط محذوف ولا بد من هذا التقدير؛ لأنه إذا كان التقدير: فليعبدوه لإيلاف قريش، تبقى الفاء\n\nولا متعلق لها. ويجوز أن يُحمل على التوكيد والفاء للتعقيب، كما يقال: لِئِلاف قريش ليعبدوه، فليعبدوا، وكذا قوله تعالى: ﴿فَلْيَفْرَحُوا﴾، وقد مر عن الزبير عن الزجاج جوازه، وعليه قوله تعالى: ﴿وَرَبَّكَ فَكَبِّرْ﴾ [المدثر: 3]، قال: \"دخلت الفاء لمعنى الشرط، كأنه قيل: وما كان فلا تدع تكبيره\".\n\nقوله: (لأن المعنى: إما لا فليعبدوه)، روي عن المصنف أنه قال: تقول العرب: افعل هذا إما لا، أي: إن كنت لا تفعل غيره فافعل هذا، و\"ما\" مزيدة، عوض من \"كان\" المحذوفة، وقد أمالوا \"لا\" لأنه ساد مسد الفعل كبلى، ولقيامهما مقام الفعل، ويقال: أعطني هذا إما لا.","html":"<p>Meccan; four verses. In the name of God, the All-Merciful, the Merciful.</p><p>On “why, then, does fāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ف</bdi>) enter?”: the fāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ف</bdi>) [in the question fa-lima] indicates objection. That is: if li-īlāf (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">لإيلاف</bdi>) depends on fa-l-yaʿbudū (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فليعبدوا</bdi>), why does the fāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ف</bdi>) of succession stand between the governing expression and what it governs? He answers that fāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ف</bdi>) introduces the consequence of an omitted condition. This must be supplied, because if one reconstructs simply “let them worship Him for the accustomed security of Quraysh,” the fāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ف</bdi>) remains without anything to which it relates.</p><p>It may also be understood as reinforcement, with fāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ف</bdi>) marking succession: “For Quraysh’s accustomed security, let them worship Him—so let them worship.” Likewise His saying “so let them rejoice” [10:58]. Its permissibility has already been transmitted from al-Zubayr, from al-Zajjāj. His saying “and your Lord—magnify Him” [74:3] follows this pattern. He says: “Fāʾ enters because of the conditional meaning, as though it said, ‘Whatever happens, do not cease to magnify Him.’”</p><p>On “the meaning is: immā (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">إمّا</bdi>) lā (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">لا</bdi>), let them worship Him”: the author is reported to have said, “The Arabs say ‘Do this, immā (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">إمّا</bdi>) lā (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">لا</bdi>),’ meaning ‘If you will not do something else, do this.’ Mā is additional, compensating for the omitted kāna. They pronounce lā (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">لا</bdi>) with imāla (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">إمالة</bdi>) because it stands in place of the verb, like balā, both taking the verb’s place. One also says, ‘Give me this, immā (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">إمّا</bdi>) lā (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">لا</bdi>).’”</p>","lemma":"فلم دخلت الفاء","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/middle-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}},{"id":"T106.02","heading":"T106.02 — Linking the two sūrahs [XVI:586]","text":"On “He made them like eaten stalks for the accustomed security of Quraysh,” al-Zajjāj says: “The meaning is: God destroyed the People of the Elephant so that Quraysh and their accustomed winter and summer journeys might remain.”\n\nOn “in the second [unit] of the sunset prayer”: that is, in the second rakʿa; in the first rakʿa he recited Sūrat “By the fig.” This apparently indicates that they are one sūrah. [This is Ṭībī’s inference, retained as his statement.]","title":"Linking the two sūrahs [XVI:586]","author":"tibi","on":["K106.01"],"anchor":"K106.01","surah":106,"ar":"قوله: (فجعلهم كعصف مأكول لإيلاف فريش)، قال الزجاج: \"المعنى: أهلك الله أصحاب الفيل، لتبقى قريش وما قد ألفوا من رحلة الشتاء والصيف\".\n\nقوله: (في الثانية من صلاة المغرب)، أي: في الركعة الثانية، وفي الركعة الأولى سورة والتين، هذا ظاهر بأنهما سورة واحدة.","html":"<p>On “He made them like eaten stalks for the accustomed security of Quraysh,” al-Zajjāj says: “The meaning is: God destroyed the People of the Elephant so that Quraysh and their accustomed winter and summer journeys might remain.”</p><p>On “in the second [unit] of the sunset prayer”: that is, in the second rakʿa; in the first rakʿa he recited Sūrat “By the fig.” This apparently indicates that they are one sūrah. [This is Ṭībī’s inference, retained as his statement.]</p>","lemma":"فجعلهم كعصف مأكول لإيلاف فريش","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/middle-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}}],"cairo_notes":[],"verses":[{"key":"106:1","number":1,"ar":"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَـٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ لِإِيلَـٰفِ قُرَيْشٍ","en":"For the taming of Qureysh"},{"key":"106:2","number":2,"ar":"إِۦلَـٰفِهِمْ رِحْلَةَ ٱلشِّتَآءِ وَٱلصَّيْفِ","en":"For their taming (We cause) the caravans to set forth in winter and summer"},{"key":"106:3","number":3,"ar":"فَلْيَعْبُدُوا۟ رَبَّ هَـٰذَا ٱلْبَيْتِ","en":"So let them worship the Lord of this House"},{"key":"106:4","number":4,"ar":"ٱلَّذِىٓ أَطْعَمَهُم مِّن جُوعٍ وَءَامَنَهُم مِّنْ خَوْفٍۭ","en":"Who hath fed them against hunger and hath made them safe from fear"}],"quran_ar":"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَـٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ لِإِيلَـٰفِ قُرَيْشٍ ﴿1﴾ إِۦلَـٰفِهِمْ رِحْلَةَ ٱلشِّتَآءِ وَٱلصَّيْفِ ﴿2﴾ فَلْيَعْبُدُوا۟ رَبَّ هَـٰذَا ٱلْبَيْتِ ﴿3﴾ ٱلَّذِىٓ أَطْعَمَهُم مِّن جُوعٍ وَءَامَنَهُم مِّنْ خَوْفٍۭ ﴿4﴾","quran_en":"1. For the taming of Qureysh 2. For their taming (We cause) the caravans to set forth in winter and summer 3. So let them worship the Lord of this House 4. Who hath fed them against hunger and hath made them safe from fear"},{"id":"K106.02","q":"106:1–2","s":106,"surah":"Quraysh","surah_ar":"قريش","title":"Quraysh · The journeys and variant forms","cairo":"K IV:800–802 · T XVI:585–590","source_page_first":800,"source_page_last":802,"chunk":"passages-106","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"The journeys and variant forms [IV:801]","html":"<p>Quraysh had two journeys: they travelled to Yemen in winter and Syria in summer, obtaining provisions and trading. They were secure on both journeys because they were the people of God’s sanctuary and custodians of His House; no one molested them, whereas other people were seized and raided.</p><p>Īlāf comes from ālaftu l-makāna (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">آلفت المكان</bdi>), ūlifuhu īlāfan (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أولفه إيلافًا</bdi>): I became accustomed to a place; hence I am muʾlif (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">مؤلف</bdi>). The poet says:\n“Among those accustomed to the [herbage / easy journey], not those grazing arāk (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أراك</bdi>).”\n[The DKA transcription reads al-rahw, “easy travelling”; Ṭībī’s printed base reads al-zahw and explains both forms below.]</p><p>The reading li-ilāfi (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">لإلاف</bdi>) Quraysh also occurs, meaning “because of Quraysh’s becoming accustomed.” It is said that one says aliftuhu ilfan wa-ilāfan (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وإلافًا</bdi>). Abū Jaʿfar reads li-ilfi Quraysh. Both forms occur in the poet’s words:\n“You claim that your brothers are Quraysh;\nthey have ilf (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">إلف</bdi>), while you have no ilāf (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">إلاف</bdi>).”</p>","author":"kashshaf","source_id":"K106.02"},{"type":"exposition","title":"The camel verse and the verbal noun [XVI:587]","html":"<p>On “among those accustomed”: one says ālaftu l-makāna (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">آلفت المكان</bdi>), ūlifuhu īlāfan (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أولفه إيلافًا</bdi>) when one becomes accustomed to a place; hence muʾlif (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">مؤلف</bdi>). [Earlier provisional reconstruction of the cited wording: al-zahw ghayra l-awārik (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الزهو غير الأوارك</bdi>). The retrieved electronic witness instead reads <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الزهو غير الإدراك</bdi>; the wording remains unresolved.] Zahw denotes being short of ripeness; zahw (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">زهو</bdi>) also means herbage and dates beginning to colour. One says zahat al-ibilu zahwan when camels travel for a night or more after watering. Zahawtuhā, “I drove them,” is also used: the verb is both transitive and intransitive. Zāhiya (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">هي</bdi>) camels do not graze saline plants. Some transmit al-rahw with rāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ر</bdi>): easy travelling. One says, “The horses came at an easy pace.” Awārik is the plural of ārika (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">آركة</bdi>): camels eating arāk (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أراك</bdi>). Al-Jawharī says: “Arakat means that she stayed among the arāk (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أراك</bdi>), which belongs to the saline vegetation; she is ārika (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">آركة</bdi>), plural awārik (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أوارك</bdi>).”</p><p>On “that is, because of Quraysh’s becoming accustomed”: according to this interpretation, ilāf (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">إلاف</bdi>) is the verbal noun of fāʿala (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فَاعَلَ</bdi>), and means muʾālafa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">مؤالفة</bdi>), like ḍāraba (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ضارب</bdi>), muḍārabatan (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">مضاربةً</bdi>) and ḍirāban (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ضرابًا</bdi>).</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T106.03"}],"types":["exposition","language","theology","reports"],"ar":"وكانت لقريش رحلتان: يرحلون في الشتاء إلى اليمن، وفي الصيف إلى الشام، فيمتارون ويتجرون، وكانوا في رحلتيهم آمنين لأنهم أهل حرم الله وولاة بيته، فلا يتعرض لهم، والناس غيرهم يتخطفون ويغار عليهم. والإيلاف من قولك: آلفت المكان أولفه إيلافا: إذا ألفته، فأنا مؤلف. قال: من المؤلفات الرهو غير الأوارك وقرئ: لئلاف قريش، أى: لمؤالفة قريش. وقيل: يقال ألفته إلفا وإلافا. وقرأ أبو جعفر: لإلف قريش، وقد جمعهما من قال: زعمتم أن إخوتكم قريش ... لهم إلف وليس لكم إلاف\n\nقوله: (من المؤلفات)، يقال: آلفت المكان أُولفه إيلافاً إذا ألفته، فأنا مؤلف. الزهو غير الإدراك، الزهو: البقل، والزهو أيضاً البُسر الملون. ويقال: زهت الإبل زهواً، إذا سارت بعد الورد ليلة وأكثر. وزهوتها أنا: يتعدى ولا يتعدى. وإبل زاهية: لا ترعى الحمض. وبعضهم يروي: الرهو بالراء، وهو السير السهل، يقال: جاءت الخيل رهواً. الأوارك جمع آركة، وهي الإبل الآكل للأراك. الجوهري: \"أركت إذا قامت في الأراك، وهي الحمض، في آركة، والجمع: أوارك\".\n\nقوله: (أي: لمؤالفة قريش)، قيل: على هذا، إلاف مصدر فاعل، فيكون بمعنى مؤالفة، نحو: ضارب مضاربة وضراباً.","layers":[{"id":"K106.02","heading":"K106.02 — The journeys and variant forms [IV:801]","text":"Quraysh had two journeys: they travelled to Yemen in winter and Syria in summer, obtaining provisions and trading. They were secure on both journeys because they were the people of God’s sanctuary and custodians of His House; no one molested them, whereas other people were seized and raided.\n\nĪlāf comes from ālaftu l-makāna (آلفت المكان), ūlifuhu īlāfan (أولفه إيلافًا): I became accustomed to a place; hence I am muʾlif (مؤلف). The poet says:\n“Among those accustomed to the [herbage / easy journey], not those grazing arāk (أراك).”\n[The DKA transcription reads al-rahw, “easy travelling”; Ṭībī’s printed base reads al-zahw and explains both forms below.]\n\nThe reading li-ilāfi (لإلاف) Quraysh also occurs, meaning “because of Quraysh’s becoming accustomed.” It is said that one says aliftuhu ilfan wa-ilāfan (وإلافًا). Abū Jaʿfar reads li-ilfi Quraysh. Both forms occur in the poet’s words:\n“You claim that your brothers are Quraysh;\nthey have ilf (إلف), while you have no ilāf (إلاف).”","title":"The journeys and variant forms [IV:801]","author":"kashshaf","surah":106,"ar":"وكانت لقريش رحلتان: يرحلون في الشتاء إلى اليمن، وفي الصيف إلى الشام، فيمتارون ويتجرون، وكانوا في رحلتيهم آمنين لأنهم أهل حرم الله وولاة بيته، فلا يتعرض لهم، والناس غيرهم يتخطفون ويغار عليهم. والإيلاف من قولك: آلفت المكان أولفه إيلافا: إذا ألفته، فأنا مؤلف. قال: من المؤلفات الرهو غير الأوارك وقرئ: لئلاف قريش، أى: لمؤالفة قريش. وقيل: يقال ألفته إلفا وإلافا. وقرأ أبو جعفر: لإلف قريش، وقد جمعهما من قال: زعمتم أن إخوتكم قريش ... لهم إلف وليس لكم إلاف","html":"<p>Quraysh had two journeys: they travelled to Yemen in winter and Syria in summer, obtaining provisions and trading. They were secure on both journeys because they were the people of God’s sanctuary and custodians of His House; no one molested them, whereas other people were seized and raided.</p><p>Īlāf comes from ālaftu l-makāna (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">آلفت المكان</bdi>), ūlifuhu īlāfan (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أولفه إيلافًا</bdi>): I became accustomed to a place; hence I am muʾlif (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">مؤلف</bdi>). The poet says:\n“Among those accustomed to the [herbage / easy journey], not those grazing arāk (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أراك</bdi>).”\n[The DKA transcription reads al-rahw, “easy travelling”; Ṭībī’s printed base reads al-zahw and explains both forms below.]</p><p>The reading li-ilāfi (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">لإلاف</bdi>) Quraysh also occurs, meaning “because of Quraysh’s becoming accustomed.” It is said that one says aliftuhu ilfan wa-ilāfan (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وإلافًا</bdi>). Abū Jaʿfar reads li-ilfi Quraysh. Both forms occur in the poet’s words:\n“You claim that your brothers are Quraysh;\nthey have ilf (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">إلف</bdi>), while you have no ilāf (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">إلاف</bdi>).”</p>","lemma":"","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/middle-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}},{"id":"T106.03","heading":"T106.03 — The camel verse and the verbal noun [XVI:587]","text":"On “among those accustomed”: one says ālaftu l-makāna (آلفت المكان), ūlifuhu īlāfan (أولفه إيلافًا) when one becomes accustomed to a place; hence muʾlif (مؤلف). [Earlier provisional reconstruction of the cited wording: al-zahw ghayra l-awārik (الزهو غير الأوارك). The retrieved electronic witness instead reads الزهو غير الإدراك; the wording remains unresolved.] Zahw denotes being short of ripeness; zahw (زهو) also means herbage and dates beginning to colour. One says zahat al-ibilu zahwan when camels travel for a night or more after watering. Zahawtuhā, “I drove them,” is also used: the verb is both transitive and intransitive. Zāhiya (هي) camels do not graze saline plants. Some transmit al-rahw with rāʾ (ر): easy travelling. One says, “The horses came at an easy pace.” Awārik is the plural of ārika (آركة): camels eating arāk (أراك). Al-Jawharī says: “Arakat means that she stayed among the arāk (أراك), which belongs to the saline vegetation; she is ārika (آركة), plural awārik (أوارك).”\n\nOn “that is, because of Quraysh’s becoming accustomed”: according to this interpretation, ilāf (إلاف) is the verbal noun of fāʿala (فَاعَلَ), and means muʾālafa (مؤالفة), like ḍāraba (ضارب), muḍārabatan (مضاربةً) and ḍirāban (ضرابًا).","title":"The camel verse and the verbal noun [XVI:587]","author":"tibi","on":["K106.02"],"anchor":"K106.02","surah":106,"ar":"قوله: (من المؤلفات)، يقال: آلفت المكان أُولفه إيلافاً إذا ألفته، فأنا مؤلف. الزهو غير الإدراك، الزهو: البقل، والزهو أيضاً البُسر الملون. ويقال: زهت الإبل زهواً، إذا سارت بعد الورد ليلة وأكثر. وزهوتها أنا: يتعدى ولا يتعدى. وإبل زاهية: لا ترعى الحمض. وبعضهم يروي: الرهو بالراء، وهو السير السهل، يقال: جاءت الخيل رهواً. الأوارك جمع آركة، وهي الإبل الآكل للأراك. الجوهري: \"أركت إذا قامت في الأراك، وهي الحمض، في آركة، والجمع: أوارك\".\n\nقوله: (أي: لمؤالفة قريش)، قيل: على هذا، إلاف مصدر فاعل، فيكون بمعنى مؤالفة، نحو: ضارب مضاربة وضراباً.","html":"<p>On “among those accustomed”: one says ālaftu l-makāna (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">آلفت المكان</bdi>), ūlifuhu īlāfan (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أولفه إيلافًا</bdi>) when one becomes accustomed to a place; hence muʾlif (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">مؤلف</bdi>). [Earlier provisional reconstruction of the cited wording: al-zahw ghayra l-awārik (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الزهو غير الأوارك</bdi>). The retrieved electronic witness instead reads <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الزهو غير الإدراك</bdi>; the wording remains unresolved.] Zahw denotes being short of ripeness; zahw (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">زهو</bdi>) also means herbage and dates beginning to colour. One says zahat al-ibilu zahwan when camels travel for a night or more after watering. Zahawtuhā, “I drove them,” is also used: the verb is both transitive and intransitive. Zāhiya (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">هي</bdi>) camels do not graze saline plants. Some transmit al-rahw with rāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ر</bdi>): easy travelling. One says, “The horses came at an easy pace.” Awārik is the plural of ārika (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">آركة</bdi>): camels eating arāk (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أراك</bdi>). Al-Jawharī says: “Arakat means that she stayed among the arāk (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أراك</bdi>), which belongs to the saline vegetation; she is ārika (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">آركة</bdi>), plural awārik (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أوارك</bdi>).”</p><p>On “that is, because of Quraysh’s becoming accustomed”: according to this interpretation, ilāf (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">إلاف</bdi>) is the verbal noun of fāʿala (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فَاعَلَ</bdi>), and means muʾālafa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">مؤالفة</bdi>), like ḍāraba (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ضارب</bdi>), muḍārabatan (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">مضاربةً</bdi>) and ḍirāban (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ضرابًا</bdi>).</p>","lemma":"من المؤلفات","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/middle-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}}],"cairo_notes":[],"verses":[{"key":"106:1","number":1,"ar":"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَـٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ لِإِيلَـٰفِ قُرَيْشٍ","en":"For the taming of Qureysh"},{"key":"106:2","number":2,"ar":"إِۦلَـٰفِهِمْ رِحْلَةَ ٱلشِّتَآءِ وَٱلصَّيْفِ","en":"For their taming (We cause) the caravans to set forth in winter and summer"}],"quran_ar":"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَـٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ لِإِيلَـٰفِ قُرَيْشٍ ﴿1﴾ إِۦلَـٰفِهِمْ رِحْلَةَ ٱلشِّتَآءِ وَٱلصَّيْفِ ﴿2﴾","quran_en":"1. For the taming of Qureysh 2. For their taming (We cause) the caravans to set forth in winter and summer"},{"id":"K106.03","q":"106:1–2","s":106,"surah":"Quraysh","surah_ar":"قريش","title":"Quraysh · The name Quraysh and the syntax of “journey”","cairo":"K IV:800–802 · T XVI:585–590","source_page_first":800,"source_page_last":802,"chunk":"passages-106","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"The name Quraysh and the syntax of “journey” [IV:802]","html":"<p>ʿIkrima reads li-yaʾlafa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ليألف</bdi>) Qurayshun, ilfahum riḥlata l-shitāʾi wa-l-ṣayf (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">رحلة الشتاء والصيف</bdi>). Quraysh are the descendants of al-Naḍr ibn Kināna. They were named with the diminutive of al-qirsh, a great sea creature that attacks ships and can be overcome only with fire. Muʿāwiya is reported to have asked Ibn ʿAbbās, “Why was Quraysh so named?” He replied, “After a sea creature that eats but is not eaten and overcomes but is not overcome,” and recited:\n“Quraysh is the creature dwelling in the sea;\nafter it Quraysh was named Quraysh.”</p><p>The diminutive conveys magnification. Another explanation derives it from qarsh, earning, because they earned through trade and travelling through the lands.</p><p>Īlāf is first stated without qualification, then replaced by an appositional expression specifying the two journeys, to magnify this accustomed security and remind them of the greatness of its blessing. Riḥla is accusative as the object of īlāfihim (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">إيلافهم</bdi>), just as yatīman (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">يتيمًا</bdi>) is made accusative by iṭʿām (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">إطعام</bdi>) [90:14–15]. The two journeys of winter and summer are meant. The singular is used because confusion is impossible, as in:\n“Eat into part of your belly [rather than fill your bellies].”</p>","author":"kashshaf","source_id":"K106.03"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Two poems [XVI:588–589]","html":"<p>On “it is also said”: this indicates a verbal noun of faʿala (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فَعَلَ</bdi>), like kataba kitāban (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">كتب كتابًا</bdi>).</p><p>After the verse beginning “You claim” comes:\n“Those people were secured against hunger and fear,\nwhereas the sons of Asad have hungered and feared.”</p><p>Its author is Musāwir ibn Hind, satirising Banū Asad. He says: you are not of Quraysh, nor are Quraysh of you; your claim to their brotherhood is false, for they were fed against hunger and secured against fear, whereas you were not. The author says: “This belongs to the verses with subtle meanings: the first hemistich reports their claim, while the second argues against them and binds them to its consequence.”</p><p>On the verse beginning “Quraysh is the creature”: al-Wāḥidī and Muḥyī l-Sunna transmit the following as al-Jumaḥī’s:\n“Quraysh is the creature dwelling in the sea;\nafter it Quraysh was named Quraysh.\nIt eats the lean and the fat, and never leaves\na feather to anything with two wings.\nSo too, throughout the lands, the tribe Quraysh\ndevours the lands in vigorous fashion.\nIn the latter age they shall have a prophet\nwho multiplies killing and scratching wounds among them.”</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T106.04"},{"type":"exposition","title":"The object and the destination [XVI:589]","html":"<p>On “just as yatīman (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">يتيمًا</bdi>) is made accusative by iṭʿām (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">إطعام</bdi>)” [90:14–15], Abū l-Baqāʾ says: “Yatīman is the object of iṭʿām (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">إطعام</bdi>). Some Basrans hold that when the verbal noun governs an object, it contains a pronoun like the pronoun within the active participle.”</p><p>On “the destination to which one travels,” al-Kawāshī says: “Originally riḥla (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">رِحْلة</bdi>) meant travelling on a riding camel; it then came to be used for every kind of travel.”</p><p>End of the sūrah [XVI:590; no further unique gloss follows the final Kashshāf block].</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T106.05"}],"types":["exposition","language","theology","reports"],"ar":"وقرأ عكرمة: ليألف قريش إلفهم رحلة الشتاء والصيف. وقريش: ولد النضر بن كنانة سموا بتصغير القرش: وهو دابة عظيمة في البحر تعبث بالسفن، ولا تطاق إلا بالنار. وعن معاوية أنه سأل ابن عباس رضى الله عنهما: بم سميت قريش؟ قال: بدابة في البحر تأكل ولا تؤكل، وتعلو ولا تعلى. وأنشد: وقريش هي التي تسكن البحر ... بها سميت قريش قريشا والتصغير للتعظيم. وقيل: من القرش وهو الكسب: لأنهم كانوا كسابين بتجاراتهم وضربهم في البلاد. أطلق الإيلاف ثم أبدل عنه المقيد بالرحلتين، تفخيما لأمر الإيلاف، وتذكيرا بعظيم النعمة فيه، ونصب الرحلة بإيلافهم مفعولا به، كما نصب يتيما بإطعام، وأراد رحلتي الشتاء والصيف، فأفرد لأمن الإلباس، كقوله. كلوا في بعض بطنكم ..\n\nقوله: (وقيل)، إشارة إلى أنه مصدر فَعَلَ، نحو: كَتَبَ كتاباً.\n\nقوله: (زعمتم) البيت، بعده: [الوافر]:\n\nأولئك أُومنوا جوعاً وخوفاً وقد جاعت بنو أسد وخافوا\n\nقائله مساور بن هند يهجو بني أسد، ويقول: إنكم لستم من قريش ولا قريش منكم، فدعواكم أخوتهم بهم باطلة؛ لأنهم أُطعموا من جوع وأُومنوا من خوف، ولستم كذلك، قال المصنف رحمه الله: وهذا من أبيات المعاني: المصراع الأول حكاية لدعواهم، والمصراع الثاني احتجاج عليهم وإلزام.\n\nقوله: (وقريش هي التي) البيت، بعده على ما رواه الواحدي ومحيي السنة للجمحي:\n\nقريش هي التي تسكن البحـ ـر، بها سميت قريش قريشاً\n\nتأكل الغث والسمين ولا تتـ ـرك يوماً لذي جناحين ريشا\n\nهكذا في البلاد حي قريش يأكلون البلاد أكلاً كميشا\n\nولهم آخر الزمان نبي يُكثر القتل فيهم والخُموشا\n\nقوله: (كما نُصب ﴿يَتِيمًا﴾، بـ ﴿إِطْعَامٌ﴾ [البلد: 14])، قال أبو البقاء: \" ﴿يَتِيمًا﴾ مفعول ﴿إِطْعَامٌ﴾، وذهب بعض البصريين إلى أن المصدر إذا عمل في المفعول، كان فيه ضمير كالضمير في اسم الفاعل\".\n\nقوله: (وهي الجهة التي يُرحل إليها)، وفي الكواشي: \"أصل الرحلة السير على الراحلة، ثم استعمل لكل سير\".\n\nتمت السورة","layers":[{"id":"K106.03","heading":"K106.03 — The name Quraysh and the syntax of “journey” [IV:802]","text":"ʿIkrima reads li-yaʾlafa (ليألف) Qurayshun, ilfahum riḥlata l-shitāʾi wa-l-ṣayf (رحلة الشتاء والصيف). Quraysh are the descendants of al-Naḍr ibn Kināna. They were named with the diminutive of al-qirsh, a great sea creature that attacks ships and can be overcome only with fire. Muʿāwiya is reported to have asked Ibn ʿAbbās, “Why was Quraysh so named?” He replied, “After a sea creature that eats but is not eaten and overcomes but is not overcome,” and recited:\n“Quraysh is the creature dwelling in the sea;\nafter it Quraysh was named Quraysh.”\n\nThe diminutive conveys magnification. Another explanation derives it from qarsh, earning, because they earned through trade and travelling through the lands.\n\nĪlāf is first stated without qualification, then replaced by an appositional expression specifying the two journeys, to magnify this accustomed security and remind them of the greatness of its blessing. Riḥla is accusative as the object of īlāfihim (إيلافهم), just as yatīman (يتيمًا) is made accusative by iṭʿām (إطعام) [90:14–15]. The two journeys of winter and summer are meant. The singular is used because confusion is impossible, as in:\n“Eat into part of your belly [rather than fill your bellies].”","title":"The name Quraysh and the syntax of “journey” [IV:802]","author":"kashshaf","surah":106,"ar":"وقرأ عكرمة: ليألف قريش إلفهم رحلة الشتاء والصيف. وقريش: ولد النضر بن كنانة سموا بتصغير القرش: وهو دابة عظيمة في البحر تعبث بالسفن، ولا تطاق إلا بالنار. وعن معاوية أنه سأل ابن عباس رضى الله عنهما: بم سميت قريش؟ قال: بدابة في البحر تأكل ولا تؤكل، وتعلو ولا تعلى. وأنشد: وقريش هي التي تسكن البحر ... بها سميت قريش قريشا والتصغير للتعظيم. وقيل: من القرش وهو الكسب: لأنهم كانوا كسابين بتجاراتهم وضربهم في البلاد. أطلق الإيلاف ثم أبدل عنه المقيد بالرحلتين، تفخيما لأمر الإيلاف، وتذكيرا بعظيم النعمة فيه، ونصب الرحلة بإيلافهم مفعولا به، كما نصب يتيما بإطعام، وأراد رحلتي الشتاء والصيف، فأفرد لأمن الإلباس، كقوله. كلوا في بعض بطنكم ..","html":"<p>ʿIkrima reads li-yaʾlafa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ليألف</bdi>) Qurayshun, ilfahum riḥlata l-shitāʾi wa-l-ṣayf (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">رحلة الشتاء والصيف</bdi>). Quraysh are the descendants of al-Naḍr ibn Kināna. They were named with the diminutive of al-qirsh, a great sea creature that attacks ships and can be overcome only with fire. Muʿāwiya is reported to have asked Ibn ʿAbbās, “Why was Quraysh so named?” He replied, “After a sea creature that eats but is not eaten and overcomes but is not overcome,” and recited:\n“Quraysh is the creature dwelling in the sea;\nafter it Quraysh was named Quraysh.”</p><p>The diminutive conveys magnification. Another explanation derives it from qarsh, earning, because they earned through trade and travelling through the lands.</p><p>Īlāf is first stated without qualification, then replaced by an appositional expression specifying the two journeys, to magnify this accustomed security and remind them of the greatness of its blessing. Riḥla is accusative as the object of īlāfihim (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">إيلافهم</bdi>), just as yatīman (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">يتيمًا</bdi>) is made accusative by iṭʿām (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">إطعام</bdi>) [90:14–15]. The two journeys of winter and summer are meant. The singular is used because confusion is impossible, as in:\n“Eat into part of your belly [rather than fill your bellies].”</p>","lemma":"","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/middle-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}},{"id":"T106.04","heading":"T106.04 — Two poems [XVI:588–589]","text":"On “it is also said”: this indicates a verbal noun of faʿala (فَعَلَ), like kataba kitāban (كتب كتابًا).\n\nAfter the verse beginning “You claim” comes:\n“Those people were secured against hunger and fear,\nwhereas the sons of Asad have hungered and feared.”\n\nIts author is Musāwir ibn Hind, satirising Banū Asad. He says: you are not of Quraysh, nor are Quraysh of you; your claim to their brotherhood is false, for they were fed against hunger and secured against fear, whereas you were not. The author says: “This belongs to the verses with subtle meanings: the first hemistich reports their claim, while the second argues against them and binds them to its consequence.”\n\nOn the verse beginning “Quraysh is the creature”: al-Wāḥidī and Muḥyī l-Sunna transmit the following as al-Jumaḥī’s:\n“Quraysh is the creature dwelling in the sea;\nafter it Quraysh was named Quraysh.\nIt eats the lean and the fat, and never leaves\na feather to anything with two wings.\nSo too, throughout the lands, the tribe Quraysh\ndevours the lands in vigorous fashion.\nIn the latter age they shall have a prophet\nwho multiplies killing and scratching wounds among them.”","title":"Two poems [XVI:588–589]","author":"tibi","on":["K106.02","K106.03"],"anchor":"K106.03","surah":106,"ar":"قوله: (وقيل)، إشارة إلى أنه مصدر فَعَلَ، نحو: كَتَبَ كتاباً.\n\nقوله: (زعمتم) البيت، بعده: [الوافر]:\n\nأولئك أُومنوا جوعاً وخوفاً وقد جاعت بنو أسد وخافوا\n\nقائله مساور بن هند يهجو بني أسد، ويقول: إنكم لستم من قريش ولا قريش منكم، فدعواكم أخوتهم بهم باطلة؛ لأنهم أُطعموا من جوع وأُومنوا من خوف، ولستم كذلك، قال المصنف رحمه الله: وهذا من أبيات المعاني: المصراع الأول حكاية لدعواهم، والمصراع الثاني احتجاج عليهم وإلزام.\n\nقوله: (وقريش هي التي) البيت، بعده على ما رواه الواحدي ومحيي السنة للجمحي:\n\nقريش هي التي تسكن البحـ ـر، بها سميت قريش قريشاً\n\nتأكل الغث والسمين ولا تتـ ـرك يوماً لذي جناحين ريشا\n\nهكذا في البلاد حي قريش يأكلون البلاد أكلاً كميشا\n\nولهم آخر الزمان نبي يُكثر القتل فيهم والخُموشا","html":"<p>On “it is also said”: this indicates a verbal noun of faʿala (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فَعَلَ</bdi>), like kataba kitāban (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">كتب كتابًا</bdi>).</p><p>After the verse beginning “You claim” comes:\n“Those people were secured against hunger and fear,\nwhereas the sons of Asad have hungered and feared.”</p><p>Its author is Musāwir ibn Hind, satirising Banū Asad. He says: you are not of Quraysh, nor are Quraysh of you; your claim to their brotherhood is false, for they were fed against hunger and secured against fear, whereas you were not. The author says: “This belongs to the verses with subtle meanings: the first hemistich reports their claim, while the second argues against them and binds them to its consequence.”</p><p>On the verse beginning “Quraysh is the creature”: al-Wāḥidī and Muḥyī l-Sunna transmit the following as al-Jumaḥī’s:\n“Quraysh is the creature dwelling in the sea;\nafter it Quraysh was named Quraysh.\nIt eats the lean and the fat, and never leaves\na feather to anything with two wings.\nSo too, throughout the lands, the tribe Quraysh\ndevours the lands in vigorous fashion.\nIn the latter age they shall have a prophet\nwho multiplies killing and scratching wounds among them.”</p>","lemma":"وقيل","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/middle-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}},{"id":"T106.05","heading":"T106.05 — The object and the destination [XVI:589]","text":"On “just as yatīman (يتيمًا) is made accusative by iṭʿām (إطعام)” [90:14–15], Abū l-Baqāʾ says: “Yatīman is the object of iṭʿām (إطعام). Some Basrans hold that when the verbal noun governs an object, it contains a pronoun like the pronoun within the active participle.”\n\nOn “the destination to which one travels,” al-Kawāshī says: “Originally riḥla (رِحْلة) meant travelling on a riding camel; it then came to be used for every kind of travel.”\n\nEnd of the sūrah [XVI:590; no further unique gloss follows the final Kashshāf block].","title":"The object and the destination [XVI:589]","author":"tibi","on":["K106.03"],"anchor":"K106.03","surah":106,"ar":"قوله: (كما نُصب ﴿يَتِيمًا﴾، بـ ﴿إِطْعَامٌ﴾ [البلد: 14])، قال أبو البقاء: \" ﴿يَتِيمًا﴾ مفعول ﴿إِطْعَامٌ﴾، وذهب بعض البصريين إلى أن المصدر إذا عمل في المفعول، كان فيه ضمير كالضمير في اسم الفاعل\".\n\nقوله: (وهي الجهة التي يُرحل إليها)، وفي الكواشي: \"أصل الرحلة السير على الراحلة، ثم استعمل لكل سير\".\n\nتمت السورة","html":"<p>On “just as yatīman (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">يتيمًا</bdi>) is made accusative by iṭʿām (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">إطعام</bdi>)” [90:14–15], Abū l-Baqāʾ says: “Yatīman is the object of iṭʿām (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">إطعام</bdi>). Some Basrans hold that when the verbal noun governs an object, it contains a pronoun like the pronoun within the active participle.”</p><p>On “the destination to which one travels,” al-Kawāshī says: “Originally riḥla (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">رِحْلة</bdi>) meant travelling on a riding camel; it then came to be used for every kind of travel.”</p><p>End of the sūrah [XVI:590; no further unique gloss follows the final Kashshāf block].</p>","lemma":"","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/middle-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}}],"cairo_notes":[],"verses":[{"key":"106:1","number":1,"ar":"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَـٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ لِإِيلَـٰفِ قُرَيْشٍ","en":"For the taming of Qureysh"},{"key":"106:2","number":2,"ar":"إِۦلَـٰفِهِمْ رِحْلَةَ ٱلشِّتَآءِ وَٱلصَّيْفِ","en":"For their taming (We cause) the caravans to set forth in winter and summer"}],"quran_ar":"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَـٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ لِإِيلَـٰفِ قُرَيْشٍ ﴿1﴾ إِۦلَـٰفِهِمْ رِحْلَةَ ٱلشِّتَآءِ وَٱلصَّيْفِ ﴿2﴾","quran_en":"1. For the taming of Qureysh 2. For their taming (We cause) the caravans to set forth in winter and summer"},{"id":"K106.04","q":"106:2–4","s":106,"surah":"Quraysh","surah_ar":"قريش","title":"Quraysh · Hunger, fear, and the concluding report","cairo":"K IV:800–802 · T XVI:585–590","source_page_first":800,"source_page_last":802,"chunk":"passages-106","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Hunger, fear, and the concluding report [IV:803]","html":"<p>Ruḥla is also read, with ḍamma (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ضَمّة</bdi>): the destination to which one travels. The indefiniteness of “hunger” and “fear” indicates their severity. Through the two journeys He fed them against the severe hunger they had previously suffered and secured them against great fear: fear of the People of the Elephant, or fear of being seized in their own country and on their routes.</p><p>It is also said that hardship had afflicted them until they ate carrion and burnt bones; and that He secured them against fear of leprosy, so that it does not afflict them in their country. All this, it is said, came through Abraham’s prayer. Among novel interpretations is: “He secured them against fear” that the caliphate might belong to others. Min khawf is also read with concealment of the nūn (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ن</bdi>) [ikhfāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">إخفاء</bdi>)].</p><p>From God’s Messenger: “Whoever recites the sūrah ‘For the accustomed security of Quraysh,’ God gives him ten good deeds for every person who circumambulates the Kaʿba and stays there in devotional retreat.”</p>","author":"kashshaf","source_id":"K106.04"}],"types":["exposition","language","theology","reports"],"ar":"وقرئ: رحلة، بالضم: وهي الجهة التي يرحل إليها: والتنكير في جوع وخوف لشدتهما، يعنى: أطعمهم بالرحلتين من جوع شديد كانوا فيه قبلهما، وآمنهم من خوف عظيم وهو خوف أصحاب الفيل، أو خوف التخطف في بلدهم ومسايرهم. وقيل: كانوا قد أصابتهم شدة حتى أكلوا الجيف والعظام المحرقة، وآمنهم من خوف الجذام فلا يصيبهم ببلدهم. وقيل ذلك كله بدعاء إبراهيم صلوات الله عليه. ومن بدع التفاسير: وآمنهم من خوف، من أن تكون الخلافة في غيرهم. وقرئ: من خوف، بإخفاء النون. عن رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم: «من قرأ سورة لإيلاف قريش أعطاه الله عشر حسنات بعدد من طاف بالكعبة واعتكف بها» .","layers":[{"id":"K106.04","heading":"K106.04 — Hunger, fear, and the concluding report [IV:803]","text":"Ruḥla is also read, with ḍamma (ضَمّة): the destination to which one travels. The indefiniteness of “hunger” and “fear” indicates their severity. Through the two journeys He fed them against the severe hunger they had previously suffered and secured them against great fear: fear of the People of the Elephant, or fear of being seized in their own country and on their routes.\n\nIt is also said that hardship had afflicted them until they ate carrion and burnt bones; and that He secured them against fear of leprosy, so that it does not afflict them in their country. All this, it is said, came through Abraham’s prayer. Among novel interpretations is: “He secured them against fear” that the caliphate might belong to others. Min khawf is also read with concealment of the nūn (ن) [ikhfāʾ (إخفاء)].\n\nFrom God’s Messenger: “Whoever recites the sūrah ‘For the accustomed security of Quraysh,’ God gives him ten good deeds for every person who circumambulates the Kaʿba and stays there in devotional retreat.”","title":"Hunger, fear, and the concluding report [IV:803]","author":"kashshaf","surah":106,"ar":"وقرئ: رحلة، بالضم: وهي الجهة التي يرحل إليها: والتنكير في جوع وخوف لشدتهما، يعنى: أطعمهم بالرحلتين من جوع شديد كانوا فيه قبلهما، وآمنهم من خوف عظيم وهو خوف أصحاب الفيل، أو خوف التخطف في بلدهم ومسايرهم. وقيل: كانوا قد أصابتهم شدة حتى أكلوا الجيف والعظام المحرقة، وآمنهم من خوف الجذام فلا يصيبهم ببلدهم. وقيل ذلك كله بدعاء إبراهيم صلوات الله عليه. ومن بدع التفاسير: وآمنهم من خوف، من أن تكون الخلافة في غيرهم. وقرئ: من خوف، بإخفاء النون. عن رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم: «من قرأ سورة لإيلاف قريش أعطاه الله عشر حسنات بعدد من طاف بالكعبة واعتكف بها» .","html":"<p>Ruḥla is also read, with ḍamma (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ضَمّة</bdi>): the destination to which one travels. The indefiniteness of “hunger” and “fear” indicates their severity. Through the two journeys He fed them against the severe hunger they had previously suffered and secured them against great fear: fear of the People of the Elephant, or fear of being seized in their own country and on their routes.</p><p>It is also said that hardship had afflicted them until they ate carrion and burnt bones; and that He secured them against fear of leprosy, so that it does not afflict them in their country. All this, it is said, came through Abraham’s prayer. Among novel interpretations is: “He secured them against fear” that the caliphate might belong to others. Min khawf is also read with concealment of the nūn (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ن</bdi>) [ikhfāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">إخفاء</bdi>)].</p><p>From God’s Messenger: “Whoever recites the sūrah ‘For the accustomed security of Quraysh,’ God gives him ten good deeds for every person who circumambulates the Kaʿba and stays there in devotional retreat.”</p>","lemma":"","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/middle-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}}],"cairo_notes":[],"verses":[{"key":"106:2","number":2,"ar":"إِۦلَـٰفِهِمْ رِحْلَةَ ٱلشِّتَآءِ وَٱلصَّيْفِ","en":"For their taming (We cause) the caravans to set forth in winter and summer"},{"key":"106:3","number":3,"ar":"فَلْيَعْبُدُوا۟ رَبَّ هَـٰذَا ٱلْبَيْتِ","en":"So let them worship the Lord of this House"},{"key":"106:4","number":4,"ar":"ٱلَّذِىٓ أَطْعَمَهُم مِّن جُوعٍ وَءَامَنَهُم مِّنْ خَوْفٍۭ","en":"Who hath fed them against hunger and hath made them safe from fear"}],"quran_ar":"إِۦلَـٰفِهِمْ رِحْلَةَ ٱلشِّتَآءِ وَٱلصَّيْفِ ﴿2﴾ فَلْيَعْبُدُوا۟ رَبَّ هَـٰذَا ٱلْبَيْتِ ﴿3﴾ ٱلَّذِىٓ أَطْعَمَهُم مِّن جُوعٍ وَءَامَنَهُم مِّنْ خَوْفٍۭ ﴿4﴾","quran_en":"2. For their taming (We cause) the caravans to set forth in winter and summer 3. So let them worship the Lord of this House 4. Who hath fed them against hunger and hath made them safe from fear"}];
